>  1.  The Guidebook suggests that there is some correlation between ExpLevel 
> and Spell Level.  Is this 1:1, like AD&D (increasing at odd-numbered levels 
> iirc), or does it vary depending on INT and/or Class?

There is a linear (well, really, stair-stepping) relationship between
XL and the difficulty of reading spellbooks.  INT and Spell Level
are also involved, and some equipment plays a factor.  Role is
irrelevant except that wizards get warned if they try to read a
spellbook which they might fail to read due it the spell being too
high level.

There is a similar (but different) relationship between XL and the
difficulty of casting spells.  There are other factors involved in
this, including spell level, skill in the spell school, either INT or
WIS (depending on your role), what armor you are wearing, and more.

>  2.  Does your Exp Level have any effect on successfully using magical items 
> such as Scrolls or Wands that have the same effect as a Spellbook-Spell? 

No.  Unlike in *D&D, these items are always used successfully (with
some caveats), and their effect is not dependent on character stats.

>  3.  Are there different Spell Level rankings of spells with the same name, 
> or do spells get any more potent as the caster's Exp Level increases...or is 
> this sort of thing any affected by Spell Proficiency?  (E.g. Do you start 

There are not; each spell has its own fixed spell level, which affects
its casting difficulty and Pw cost, among other things.

Some spells get more potent as caster XL increases (most damaging
spells gain dice every odd level)

Spell skills improve the effectiveness of some spells, often by giving
them an additional related effect   Mostly spell skills make it easier
to cast higher level spells within their school.

>  1.  The Guidebook doesn't mention it, but I suspect that Spell Types are 
> linked to character Classes in some manner. (I read somewhere that each 
> class as one particular spell that they specialize it, but that might just 
> be a rumor.)  After reading the Guidebook my imagination reeled with the 
> possibilities of Wizards casting powerful healing spells, Clerics showering 
> the battlefield with Magic Missiles, and lowly Tourists summoning forth 
> arch-fiends from the nether planes.  But, I doubt the game actually works 
> that way or what would be the point of playing one class over another?

Each role has a list of the maximum skill level to which it can
advance each skill (this is true for weapon skills as well as spell
skills).  Many skills are "restricted" and may not even be advanced to
Basic.  However, there is nothing to stop you from trying to find a
way to get your Priest to learn magic missile and get the failure
chance down into an acceptable range if you want to.  Many of these
sorts of things are possible, if more difficult when you try to
deviate from role expectations.  (Similarly, you can have a Wizard
swinging a giant two-handed axe if you want.)

